WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.138 --> 00:00:16.222
Marilla walked home one late April evening and realized happily that winter was over.

2
00:00:16.962 --> 00:00:21.986
She looked affectionately at Green Gables, and looked forward to a warm fire and a cup of tea

3
00:00:22.026 --> 00:00:22.526
with Anne.

4
00:00:23.087 --> 00:00:27.590
When she got home, the fire wasn't lit, and there was no sign of Anne anywhere.

5
00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.552
Marilla was disappointed and irritated.

6
00:00:31.216 --> 00:00:36.119
By the time Marilla got dinner ready, it was dark, and Anne had still not come home.

7
00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.683
Marilla went upstairs and found Anne lying on the bed, face down among the pillows.

8
00:00:42.703 --> 00:00:44.324
"My goodness!"

9
00:00:44.905 --> 00:00:46.325
said the astonished Marilla.

10
00:00:46.345 --> 00:00:48.507
"Have you been asleep, Anne?"

11
00:00:48.527 --> 00:00:51.288
"No," was the muffled reply.

12
00:00:51.308 --> 00:00:52.869
"Are you sick then?"

13
00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:54.490
asked Marilla anxiously.

14
00:00:55.171 --> 00:00:56.832
Marilla moved closer to the bed.

15
00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.263
"No. But please, Marilla, go away and don't look at me.

16
00:01:01.844 --> 00:01:04.686
I'll never be able to go anywhere again."

17
00:01:04.706 --> 00:01:07.228
"Anne Shirley, what's the matter?"

18
00:01:07.648 --> 00:01:08.309
said Marilla.

19
00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:09.890
Anne turned over.

20
00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:13.733
"Look at my hair, Marilla," she whispered.

21
00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:16.976
Marilla lifted her candle and looked closely.

22
00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:19.838
Anne's hair certainly looked very strange.

23
00:01:19.858 --> 00:01:22.901
"Anne, what have you done?

24
00:01:23.682 --> 00:01:26.144
Why, your hair is green!"

25
00:01:27.324 --> 00:01:28.505
And it was green.

26
00:01:29.205 --> 00:01:34.529
Never in all her life had Marilla seen anything so ugly as Anne's hair at that moment.

27
00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:37.971
"Yes, it's green," moaned Anne.

28
00:01:37.991 --> 00:01:41.414
"I thought nothing could be as bad as red hair.

29
00:01:41.994 --> 00:01:45.977
But now I know it's ten times worse to have green hair."

30
00:01:45.997 --> 00:01:53.602
"Anne," Marilla said, "I've been expecting something to happen because you haven't done anything strange for two months!

31
00:01:54.342 --> 00:01:56.504
How did your hair turn green?"

32
00:01:57.592 --> 00:01:58.412
"I dyed it."

33
00:01:58.432 --> 00:01:59.433
"Dyed it?

34
00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:01.733
You dyed your hair?"

35
00:02:02.393 --> 00:02:04.154
Marilla looked at Anne sternly.

36
00:02:04.174 --> 00:02:10.456
"Well," continued Marilla sarcastically, "I would have dyed it a decent color, at least.

37
00:02:11.056 --> 00:02:12.536
I wouldn't have dyed it green."

38
00:02:12.556 --> 00:02:18.558
"He said it would turn my hair a beautiful raven black," protested Anne.

39
00:02:18.578 --> 00:02:20.278
"Who said that?"

40
00:02:20.298 --> 00:02:23.139
"The peddler that was here this afternoon.

41
00:02:23.859 --> 00:02:25.299
I bought the dye from him."

42
00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:27.100
"Anne Shirley!

43
00:02:27.696 --> 00:02:29.677
This is what your vanity has done.

44
00:02:30.338 --> 00:02:33.399
Now give your hair a good wash," Marilla said.

45
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.481
Anne washed her hair, but it made no difference.

46
00:02:36.501 --> 00:02:39.343
"Oh Marilla, what can I do?

47
00:02:39.963 --> 00:02:43.625
People have forgotten my other mistakes—shouting at Mrs.

48
00:02:43.725 --> 00:02:51.269
Lynde, making Diana drunk, jumping on Miss Barry, and cracking a slate on Gilbert Blythe.

49
00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:54.131
But they'll never forget this one."

50
00:02:55.091 --> 00:02:59.262
For the next week, Anne went nowhere and washed her hair every day.

51
00:03:00.003 --> 00:03:03.706
At the end of the week, Marilla said firmly, "It's no use, Anne.

52
00:03:04.306 --> 00:03:06.468
Your hair must be cut off."

53
00:03:07.108 --> 00:03:08.529
Anne nodded and wept.

54
00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:12.893
Marilla's haircut was very thorough, and the result was not pretty.

55
00:03:13.753 --> 00:03:17.656
Anne's hair was quite short, and it made a sensation in school on Monday.

56
00:03:18.297 --> 00:03:22.280
But to her relief, nobody guessed the real reason behind her haircut.

57
00:03:22.952 --> 00:03:26.715
However, Josie Pye did tell Anne she looked like a scarecrow.

58
00:03:27.376 --> 00:03:30.278
A few weeks later, the girls were standing at the brook.

59
00:03:30.979 --> 00:03:32.560
In the brook was a small boat.

60
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.582
It was Anne's idea that they act out a poem.

61
00:03:35.602 --> 00:03:39.225
"You must be the Lily Maid, Anne," said Diana.

62
00:03:39.245 --> 00:03:42.147
"I'm not brave enough to float down the river."

63
00:03:42.167 --> 00:03:47.872
"But it's ridiculous to have a redheaded Lily Maid," said Anne.

64
00:03:47.892 --> 00:03:52.576
"Your hair is much darker since you cut it," said Diana earnestly.

65
00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.481
"Really?" exclaimed Anne, flushing with delight.

66
00:03:57.141 --> 00:03:59.742
Then she went back to deciding the parts for their skit.

67
00:03:59.762 --> 00:04:03.263
"I'll be the Lily Maid," she agreed reluctantly.

68
00:04:03.283 --> 00:04:07.864
"Ruby, you be King Arthur, and Diana must be Lancelot."

69
00:04:08.644 --> 00:04:13.285
Anne lay down in the boat, closed her eyes and folded her hands on her chest.

70
00:04:13.305 --> 00:04:17.367
"She really looks dead," whispered Ruby Gillis.

71
00:04:18.147 --> 00:04:22.208
Instead of lilies Diana placed a posy of violets in Anne's hands.

72
00:04:23.064 --> 00:04:25.186
"Now she's ready," said Ruby.

73
00:04:25.886 --> 00:04:29.108
The girls kissed the "Lily Maid" and pushed the boat off.

74
00:04:29.909 --> 00:04:34.872
For a few minutes, as the boat slowly drifted, Anne enjoyed the romance of her journey.

75
00:04:35.613 --> 00:04:37.994
Then something very unromantic happened.

76
00:04:38.574 --> 00:04:39.855
The boat began to leak.

77
00:04:41.056 --> 00:04:45.379
The Lily Maid was forced to get up and look at the big crack, through which water was

78
00:04:45.519 --> 00:04:50.903
pouring. Anne gave a gasping little scream that nobody heard, and then she went pale.

79
00:04:51.563 --> 00:04:52.524
But she stayed calm.

80
00:04:53.248 --> 00:04:55.430
There was one chance—just one.

81
00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:01.174
Anne prayed that the boat would keep drifting toward the bridge and float near one of its columns.

82
00:05:01.815 --> 00:05:05.738
The columns were made of old tree trunks, with branch stubs that she could sit on.

83
00:05:06.758 --> 00:05:08.199
Anne's prayer was answered.

84
00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.763
The boat drifted under the bridge, and Anne scrambled onto a column.

85
00:05:13.383 --> 00:05:16.486
She sat there, with no way of getting up or down.

86
00:05:17.166 --> 00:05:19.368
The boat kept drifting and then sank.

87
00:05:20.036 --> 00:05:25.060
Ruby and Diana waited farther down the brook, and they saw the boat disappear underwater.

88
00:05:25.741 --> 00:05:29.103
They went white as sheets, shrieked, and ran off.

89
00:05:30.124 --> 00:05:35.208
For the unlucky Lily Maid, the minutes ticked by, each one like an hour.

90
00:05:35.909 --> 00:05:37.150
Why didn't someone come?

91
00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:39.131
Where had her friends gone?

92
00:05:39.832 --> 00:05:41.053
Suppose nobody came!

93
00:05:41.714 --> 00:05:46.658
Just when Anne thought she could hold on no longer, Gilbert Blythe rowed toward her.

94
00:05:46.678 --> 00:05:48.179
"Anne Shirley!

95
00:05:48.519 --> 00:05:49.460
How did you get there?"

96
00:05:49.876 --> 00:05:50.616
he exclaimed.

97
00:05:51.296 --> 00:05:52.837
He helped Anne into his boat.

98
00:05:53.537 --> 00:05:56.198
She sat there, furious and soaking wet.

99
00:05:56.858 --> 00:05:57.838
"What happened?"

100
00:05:58.278 --> 00:05:59.019
asked Gilbert.

101
00:05:59.039 --> 00:06:05.220
"We were playing, and I was the Lily Maid," Anne said, not even looking at Gilbert.

102
00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:09.562
"I had to float to Camelot—I mean, down the brook.

103
00:06:10.202 --> 00:06:12.662
The boat leaked, and I had to get out.

104
00:06:13.243 --> 00:06:14.863
Can you row me to the bank?"

105
00:06:15.343 --> 00:06:15.923
asked Anne.

106
00:06:16.664 --> 00:06:17.664
Gilbert obliged.

107
00:06:18.376 --> 00:06:21.498
"I'm very grateful," said Anne haughtily.

108
00:06:21.518 --> 00:06:26.140
"Anne," he said quickly, "I'm sorry I made fun of your hair.

109
00:06:26.801 --> 00:06:27.761
It was only a joke.

110
00:06:28.401 --> 00:06:30.503
Besides, it was so long ago.

111
00:06:30.663 --> 00:06:31.563
Can't we be friends?"

112
00:06:32.364 --> 00:06:34.085
For a moment Anne hesitated.

113
00:06:34.725 --> 00:06:37.126
Her heart gave a strange, quick little beat.

114
00:06:37.807 --> 00:06:42.349
But then the scene of two years ago flashed in her head, like it had happened yesterday.

115
00:06:43.049 --> 00:06:44.430
She hated Gilbert Blythe!

116
00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:46.351
She would never forgive him!

117
00:06:46.371 --> 00:06:49.077
"No," she said coldly.

118
00:06:49.097 --> 00:06:52.360
"I will never be friends with you, Gilbert Blythe."

119
00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:53.721
"All right!"

120
00:06:54.301 --> 00:06:56.984
Gilbert went down to his boat, his cheeks red.

121
00:06:57.004 --> 00:07:00.447
"I'll never ask you to be friends again, Anne Shirley.

122
00:07:00.967 --> 00:07:02.108
And I don't care either!"

123
00:07:02.789 --> 00:07:04.350
He rowed away furiously.

124
00:07:05.031 --> 00:07:09.715
As Anne walked home, she held her head high, but she was conscious of an odd
